  "source_evidence": [
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equation (1.1) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q_t(x,t) + A q_x(x,t) = 0; constant-coefficient linear case",
      "observation": "The setting is a homogeneous first-order system in one spatial dimension, with q: R × R → R^m and A a constant real m × m matrix. Subscripts denote partial derivatives."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 23; printed page 1; equations (1.2)–(1.3) and following paragraph",
      "anchor": "q(x,t) = q̃(x − ū t); propagates with constant speed ū and unchanged shape",
      "observation": "Scalar advection supplies the introductory meaning of propagation: a profile translates at the scalar coefficient's speed without changing shape."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; equation (1.4) and preceding paragraph",
      "anchor": "to the right with speed c > 0",
      "observation": "The equation w_t + c w_x = 0 models a right-moving wave when c is positive."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; equations (1.5)–(1.6)",
      "anchor": "q = [p,u]^T; A = [[0,K],[1/ρ,0]]",
      "observation": "The acoustics example uses pressure perturbation p, particle velocity u, constant bulk modulus K, and constant density ρ."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 24; printed page 2; characteristic combinations following equation (1.6)",
      "anchor": "w¹ = p + ρcu; w² = p − ρcu; c = √(K/ρ)",
      "observation": "The source states that w¹ satisfies w¹_t + c w¹_x = 0 and w² satisfies w²_t − c w²_x = 0. The second wave propagates to the left with propagation speed −c."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "hyperbolic if the matrix A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors",
      "observation": "Hyperbolicity in the selected constant-coefficient setting requires real eigenvalues and a complete set of linearly independent eigenvectors."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "any vector in R^m can be uniquely decomposed as a linear combination of these eigenvectors",
      "observation": "The source explicitly asserts unique vector decomposition in the selected eigenvector basis and connects it to decomposition into distinct waves, referring to Chapter 3."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; opening paragraph; selected sentence",
      "anchor": "The corresponding eigenvalues of A give the wave speeds at which each wave propagates.",
      "observation": "Each component wave has propagation speed equal to the eigenvalue corresponding to its eigenvector."
    },
    {
      "location": "Raw PDF page 25; printed page 3; end of opening paragraph",
      "anchor": "the acoustics matrix A of (1.6) has eigenvalues −c and +c",
      "observation": "The source illustrates the general claim using the two signed acoustic propagation speeds."
    }
  ],
  "statement": {
    "binders": [
      "The inherited setting has one spatial coordinate x ∈ R and time coordinate t ∈ R, with q: R × R → R^m. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.1) and following paragraph.]",
      "A is a constant real m × m matrix; m is the number of components of q. The source uses arbitrary m without an explicit numerical range. [Printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1).]",
      "Choose a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors of A with real eigenvalues. The claim applies to each wave in the resulting decomposition; the selected passage introduces no indexing notation. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph, hyperbolicity definition and selected sentence.]"
    ],
    "hypotheses": [
      "The governing equation is the homogeneous, one-dimensional, constant-coefficient linear system q_t + A q_x = 0. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.1) and surrounding text; printed p. 3 explicitly refers back to system (1.1).]",
      "The system is hyperbolic in the source's stated sense: A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding set of m linearly independent eigenvectors. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]"
    ],
    "conclusions": [
      "Every vector in R^m has a unique expansion as a linear combination of the chosen eigenvectors. This is an explicit supporting claim in the enclosing paragraph. [Printed p. 3, sentence beginning 'This means that any vector'.]",
      "The eigenvector decomposition provides a decomposition into component waves, with the fuller account deferred to Chapter 3. [Printed p. 3, sentence beginning 'As we will see in Chapter 3'.]",
      "Each component wave propagates with the speed given by its corresponding eigenvalue of A. [Printed p. 3, selected sentence beginning 'The corresponding eigenvalues of A'.]",
      "As an explicit example, the acoustic matrix in (1.6) has eigenvalues −c and +c, which are its acoustic wave propagation speeds. [Printed p. 3, final two sentences of the opening paragraph.]"
    ],
    "definitions_and_conventions": [
      "q_t and q_x denote partial derivatives with respect to t and x. [Printed p. 1, paragraph following (1.1).]",
      "Hyperbolicity here includes a complete eigenvector basis, rather than merely reality of the eigenvalues. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph, definition of 'hyperbolic'.]",
      "Propagation speed is signed: positive c gives rightward propagation, while −c gives leftward propagation. [Printed p. 2, text preceding (1.4) and final paragraph.]",
      "Scalar propagation is illustrated by q(x,t) = q̃(x − ū t), a translation of an unchanged profile. [Printed p. 1, equations (1.2)–(1.3) and following paragraph.]",
      "In the acoustic example, A = [[0,K],[1/ρ,0]], c = √(K/ρ), and the scalar combinations are w¹ = p + ρcu and w² = p − ρcu. [Printed p. 2, equation (1.6) and subsequent displayed formulas.]"
    ],
    "implicit_context": [
      "The selected claim concerns the constant-coefficient system (1.1), as explicitly identified in its enclosing paragraph. The later conservation-law equation and variable flux Jacobian are introduced separately. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph and section 1.1, equations (1.8)–(1.9).]",
      "The two acoustic combinations satisfying scalar equations serve as the preceding model for the general wave decomposition. [Printed p. 2, formulas after (1.6); printed p. 3, opening sentence.]",
      "The source distinguishes wave propagation speed from material particle velocity: acoustic waves can travel much faster than the material particles. [Printed p. 2, opening paragraph.]"
    ],
    "semantic_facets": [
      {
        "topic": "Eigenvalue-to-wave correspondence",
        "details": [
          "The speed is the eigenvalue corresponding to the component's eigenvector, so the statement preserves a correspondence between components and speeds. [Printed p. 3, unique eigenvector expansion followed by the selected sentence.]",
          "The source does not require pairwise distinct eigenvalues in its definition of hyperbolicity. Its phrase 'distinct waves' therefore does not itself impose distinct propagation speeds. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph, hyperbolicity definition and subsequent sentences.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Meaning of propagation",
        "details": [
          "The introductory scalar model realizes propagation by translation of a profile with unchanged shape. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.3) and following paragraph.]",
          "Negative propagation speeds encode direction and must not be replaced by absolute values. [Printed p. 2, final paragraph; printed p. 3, acoustic eigenvalues −c and +c.]"
        ]
      },
      {
        "topic": "Scope of the assertion",
        "details": [
          "The selected sentence is an explanatory claim in introductory prose, supported by the eigenvector-basis statement and acoustic example, rather than a separately quantified theorem. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]",
          "No initial data, boundary conditions, solution regularity class, or existence-and-uniqueness theorem for the PDE is specified in this paragraph. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph; printed p. 1, equation (1.1).]"
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "undebatable_constraints": [
    "Retain the constant real matrix and homogeneous linear PDE context. [Printed p. 1, equation (1.1); printed p. 3, explicit reference to system (1.1).]",
    "Retain both real eigenvalues and m linearly independent eigenvectors as the stated hyperbolicity conditions. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]",
    "Preserve the correspondence between each component wave and its eigenvalue as propagation speed. [Printed p. 3, selected sentence.]",
    "Preserve signed direction of propagation, including the acoustic speeds −c and +c. [Printed p. 2, final paragraph; printed p. 3, acoustic example.]",
    "Do not infer pairwise distinct eigenvalues from the phrase 'distinct waves'; the stated hyperbolicity definition imposes no such condition. [Printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]",
    "The unique decomposition is relative to the chosen eigenvector basis; the passage does not assert uniqueness of the choice of basis. [Printed p. 3, phrase 'these eigenvectors'.]"
  ],
  "ambiguities": [
    {
      "issue": "The general component waves are not explicitly defined by a formula in the selected passage.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 3 connects the eigenvector expansion to waves with 'As we will see in Chapter 3'; printed pp. 1–2 provide scalar translation and acoustic examples.",
      "impact": "The eigenvalue-speed claim is explicit, but its general characteristic-coordinate formulation is not supplied on the attached pages. The referenced Chapter 3 account has not been inspected in this extraction."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The solution class and differentiability assumptions are unstated.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 1 introduces partial derivatives in (1.1) and describes (1.3) as a solution for 'any function' q̃; printed p. 3 states the general wave-speed claim without regularity hypotheses.",
      "impact": "The source does not resolve classical versus weak interpretation for arbitrary profiles. An exact smoothness assumption cannot be attributed to the selected passage."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The term 'distinct waves' does not specify how repeated eigenvalues are handled.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 3 requires real eigenvalues and an eigenvector basis, then describes a decomposition into 'distinct waves', without requiring distinct eigenvalues.",
      "impact": "A basis-dependent decomposition with coincident speeds is not excluded; no canonical decomposition within a repeated-eigenvalue subspace is asserted."
    },
    {
      "issue": "The acoustic parameter restrictions are conveyed physically rather than fully stated as inequalities.",
      "evidence": "Printed p. 2 identifies K as bulk modulus and ρ as density, defines c = √(K/ρ), and earlier describes rightward sound propagation with c > 0.",
      "impact": "The physical acoustic example supplies the ±c interpretation, but these pages do not explicitly enumerate algebraic assumptions such as K > 0 and ρ > 0."
    }
  ],
  "contract_plain_english": "For the homogeneous one-dimensional constant-coefficient real system q_t + A q_x = 0, assume A has real eigenvalues and a corresponding basis of m eigenvectors. The source states that every vector has a unique expansion in this basis and that this yields a decomposition into waves, each propagating at the signed speed equal to its corresponding eigenvalue. The acoustic example has speeds −c and +c, representing leftward and rightward waves. The general decomposition is explained further by a reference to Chapter 3; the attached passage does not specify a solution regularity class or a general component formula. [Printed p. 1, (1.1); printed p. 2, acoustic scalar equations and directional interpretation; printed p. 3, opening paragraph.]"
}
